This uses a dictionary to keep the shallowest node for each horizontal distance. The recursive walk checks every node, but replacement happens only when the new node is closer to the root.

After that, the dictionary keys are sorted. Reading them in order gives the view from the far left column to the far right column. The tree itself is not modified.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * public class TreeNode {
 *     int data;
 *     TreeNode left;
 *     TreeNode right;
 *     TreeNode(int val) { data = val; left = null; right = null; }
 * }
 **/

public class Solution
{
    public List<int> TopView(TreeNode root)
    {
        var answer = new List<int>();
        if (root == null)
        {
            return answer;
        }

        var columns = new Dictionary<int, NodeInfo>();
        Visit(root, 0, 0, columns);

        var distances = new List<int>(columns.Keys);
        distances.Sort();

        foreach (int distance in distances)
        {
            answer.Add(columns[distance].Value);
        }
        return answer;
    }

    private void Visit(TreeNode node, int distance, int depth,
                       Dictionary<int, NodeInfo> columns)
    {
        if (node == null)
        {
            return;
        }

        if (!columns.ContainsKey(distance) || depth < columns[distance].Depth)
        {
            columns[distance] = new NodeInfo(depth, node.data);
        }

        Visit(node.left, distance - 1, depth + 1, columns);
        Visit(node.right, distance + 1, depth + 1, columns);
    }

    private class NodeInfo
    {
        public int Depth { get; }
        public int Value { get; }

        public NodeInfo(int depth, int value)
        {
            Depth = depth;
            Value = value;
        }
    }
}
```

One edge case is a skewed tree. Even then, every node gets a different or repeated column distance, and sorting the keys still returns the correct order. Very deep trees can make recursion use a lot of call stack space.

Time complexity: O(n + k log k), where k is the number of columns. Space complexity: O(n), including the dictionary and recursion stack.

This version uses recursion. For every horizontal distance, it stores the node with the smallest depth found so far. A node is visible only when no node above it has already claimed that distance.

The root has distance 0. Moving left subtracts 1, and moving right adds 1. If the tree is empty, the method returns an empty list.

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;

/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * public class TreeNode {
 *     int data;
 *     TreeNode left;
 *     TreeNode right;
 *     TreeNode(int val) { data = val; left = null; right = null; }
 * }
 **/

class Solution {
    public List<Integer> topView(TreeNode root) {
        List<Integer> answer = new ArrayList<>();
        if (root == null) {
            return answer;
        }

        Map<Integer, int[]> best = new HashMap<>();
        visit(root, 0, 0, best);

        List<Integer> distances = new ArrayList<>(best.keySet());
        Collections.sort(distances);
        for (int distance : distances) {
            answer.add(best.get(distance)[1]);
        }
        return answer;
    }

    private void visit(TreeNode node, int distance, int depth,
                       Map<Integer, int[]> best) {
        if (node == null) {
            return;
        }

        if (!best.containsKey(distance) || depth < best.get(distance)[0]) {
            best.put(distance, new int[] {depth, node.data});
        }

        visit(node.left, distance - 1, depth + 1, best);
        visit(node.right, distance + 1, depth + 1, best);
    }
}
