A small dry run: the root records `(distance 0, depth 0)`. A node two levels down at distance 0 cannot replace it, because the root is already higher.

Time complexity: O(n + k log k), where k is the number of horizontal distances. Space complexity: O(n), including the recursion stack and map.

A queue keeps the tree in top-to-bottom order. Each node also carries its horizontal distance from the root. The first node seen at a distance is the one visible from above, so later nodes at the same distance are ignored.

For example, if two nodes have horizontal distance 1, the one found at the smaller level is stored. This code does not change the tree.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * struct TreeNode {
 *     int data;
 *     TreeNode *left;
 *     TreeNode *right;
 *     TreeNode(int val) : data(val), left(nullptr), right(nullptr) {}
 * };
 **/

class Solution{
    public:
    vector<int> topView(TreeNode *root){
        vector<int> answer;
        if (root == nullptr) {
            return answer;
        }

        map<int, int> firstAtDistance;
        queue<pair<TreeNode*, int>> nodes;
        nodes.push({root, 0});

        while (!nodes.empty()) {
            auto [current, distance] = nodes.front();
            nodes.pop();

            if (!firstAtDistance.count(distance)) {
                firstAtDistance[distance] = current->data;
            }

            if (current->left != nullptr) {
                nodes.push({current->left, distance - 1});
            }
            if (current->right != nullptr) {
                nodes.push({current->right, distance + 1});
            }
        }

        for (const auto& entry : firstAtDistance) {
            answer.push_back(entry.second);
        }
        return answer;
    }
};
```

A dry run starts with the root at distance 0. Its left child gets -1 and its right child gets 1. The map is finally read from the smallest distance to the largest distance.

Time complexity: O(n log n) because the distances are stored in an ordered map. Space complexity: O(n).

The tree is visited one level at a time. At each level, only the first node found for a horizontal distance is kept. Since levels are processed from top to bottom, that first node is the visible one.

The map does not give the final left-to-right order, so the collected distances are sorted before building the result. A nil root is handled at the start.

```go
package main

import "sort"

/*
type TreeNode struct {
    data int
    left *TreeNode
    right *TreeNode
}
*/

func topView(root *TreeNode) []int {
	if root == nil {
		return []int{}
	}

	type item struct {
		node     *TreeNode
		distance int
	}

	currentLevel := []item{{root, 0}}
	visible := make(map[int]int)

	for len(currentLevel) > 0 {
		nextLevel := make([]item, 0)
		for _, current := range currentLevel {
			if _, exists := visible[current.distance]; !exists {
				visible[current.distance] = current.node.data
			}

			if current.node.left != nil {
				nextLevel = append(nextLevel, item{current.node.left, current.distance - 1})
			}
			if current.node.right != nil {
				nextLevel = append(nextLevel, item{current.node.right, current.distance + 1})
			}
		}
		currentLevel = nextLevel
	}

	distances := make([]int, 0, len(visible))
	for distance := range visible {
		distances = append(distances, distance)
	}
	sort.Ints(distances)

	answer := make([]int, 0, len(distances))
	for _, distance := range distances {
		answer = append(answer, visible[distance])
	}
	return answer
}
```

