## Approach
The idea is to assign every node a row (depth) and column (horizontal distance from the root). The root starts at (0, 0), the left child is placed at (row + 1, col - 1), and the right child at (row + 1, col + 1). For the top view, among all nodes that lie in the same column, we need the one that is closest to the root, i.e., the node with the smallest row value (minimum depth). During a DFS traversal, a map is used to store, for each column, the node with the minimum row encountered so far. Whenever a node in the same column is found at a smaller depth, it replaces the previously stored node. After the traversal, iterating through the map from leftmost to rightmost column gives the nodes visible in the top view of the binary tree.

